# Build artifacts
/target/

# Runtime data: nothing a run produces belongs in version control.
# The directories themselves are kept via .gitkeep files.
/output/*
!/output/.gitkeep
/logs/*
!/logs/.gitkeep

# Editor / backup files
**/*.rs.bk
*.swp
/.freebuff

# Python bytecode caches. These are build products of importing the bundled
# scripts, not source, and must never be committed: a stale .pyc shadows the
# .py it was built from and makes a fixed script look broken.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# What wireless tools leave in the working directory when they start. Kismet
# names its capture after the moment it began, so a check run leaves one behind
# every time and they are never wanted in a repository.
/Kismet-*.kismet
/Kismet-*.kismet-journal
*.pcapng
*.pcap

# What an OSINT or payload tool drops in the working directory when it runs.
# maigret names its report after the handle it searched unless told otherwise.
/reports/report_*


# Secrets
.env

# Wordlists too large for a git repository. Both GitHub and Codeberg refuse
# blobs over 100 MB, and rockyou alone is 140 MB. Run
# wordlists/fetch-wordlists.sh to obtain them; the pinned SHA-256 for each is
# in wordlists/MANIFEST.tsv and ./verify-wordlists.sh checks it.
# Everything else under wordlists/ is committed.
/wordlists/passwords/rockyou.txt
/wordlists/passwords/openwall.txt
/wordlists/users/top-1m.txt
/wordlists/web/dirbuster-big.txt
/wordlists/**/*.part

# ── run output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing a capability run produces belongs in version control, whatever wrote
# it. These are the shapes the catalog's tools actually emit when an operation
# writes to a bare filename and therefore lands in the working directory it was
# launched from — which, for a run, is this repository. Eleven such files were
# committed once; the rules below exist so a run cannot leave a tracked change
# behind even when everything else it does is right.
#
# Scanner default reports.
admin.txt
cookies.txt
dirb_out.txt
endpoints.txt
urls.txt
archive_urls.txt
results.txt
report.txt
output.txt
names.txt
subdomains.txt
subs.txt
domains.txt
hosts.txt
live_hosts.txt
open_ports.txt
found.txt
hits.txt
/ferox_content.json
/ferox_dirs.json
/sslscan_out.xml
/sslscan_*.xml
/nmap_output.xml
/gitleaks_report.json
/gitleaks-report.json
/trivy-report.json
/grype-report.json
/zgrab2_out.csv
/subfinder_output.txt
/hakrawler_urls.txt
/katana.txt
/gospider.json

# A tool handed a bare name and told to create a file by that name, e.g. a
# scanner writing example.com.txt after the host it was pointed at.
example.com.txt
*.${TARGET}.txt

# Every run writes into its own directory under output/, and every build into
# dist/. Neither is source. These are listed so that a file created by mistake
# at the repository root — a scanner handed a bare output filename, a payload
# builder run from the wrong directory, a tool that ignored its working
# directory — is ignored by default instead of needing a new entry each time.
#
# The rule is deliberately blunt: anything that looks like a generated report,
# a harvested result, a captured artefact or a build product is not source.
# Adding a new entry per incident meant the same incident recurring, because
# `git add -A` ran before anyone noticed what it was staging.
/output/
/dist/
/build/
/Result/
/logs/
/screenshots/
/tmp/

*.json_*
/connect_timeout=*
/nmap_segfault_*
/sslscan_*.xml
/_*/                       # PyInstaller extraction dirs left by msfvenom &c

# Harvested and captured data. These are findings about someone else's host.
*.har
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.cap
*.22000
*.hc22000
*.22
*.12
*.kismet.csv
*_content.json
*_params.json
*_vhost.json
*_api.json
*_param.json
*_path.json
*_out.json
*_results.json
*_report.json
*_vuln.json
*_exec.json
*_*.json.example.com

# Generated payloads and binaries. The catalog generates these into the run's
# own directory; a copy at the root is a mistake, not a fixture.
*.enc
*.zip
*.doc
*.docm
*.elf
*.php
*.jsp
*.war
*.dll
*.exe
*.bin
*.raw
*.sh_evil
/cert.pem
/key.pem
/decrypted_*

# A build product of the project's own packaging step, and anything else a
# build leaves lying about. Packaging sources live under packaging/, so no
# .spec file at the root is ever source.
/*.spec
*.spec.bak

# A scanner handed a bare filename writes it into its working directory, and a
# file named after the host it looked at is the common case: `curl example.com
# > example.com`, `zgrab2 ... -o example.com`, a scan named for the target with
# a timestamp. Anchored at the root so a real directory of the same name is not
# swallowed.
/example.com
/example.com*
/chuka.ac.ke*
/target.*
/live_hosts*
/hosts.txt
/subdomains.txt

# Test and diagnostic harnesses belong in a scratch directory, not the source
# tree. `cargo test` unit tests live inside src/ and are source; integration
# suites and probe scripts belong under /tmp.
/tests/
/harness*.py
/*_run.py
/fixinputs.py
/addinput.py
/check_flags.py

# The committed record of the audit is a real project artefact and is kept.
# A harvested report written at the root. The committed record of the audit is
# a real project artefact and stays; this is the loose file beside it.
/reports.json
